Tam—to him; badhvaa—by binding; virathi-kritya—deprived him of the chariot; krrichhena—by difficulty; kuravah—the Kurus;
yudh—in battle; kumaram—a young man or child; swasya—his own; kanyam—daughter; cha—and; swa-puram—his own city;
jayinah—the victors; avishan—entered. During the war, after making Samba without a chariot, the Kuru warriors with great difficulty tied him up.
And then they, taking that prince and their princess, entered their city with a victorious feeling. Hearing this, Narada, the king was very pleased. Kurunpratyudyamam chakrurugrasanaprachoditaah. 1. Meaning of the word

Tat—This; Shrutva—Having heard; Narada—Of Narada Muni; Uktena—From the narrations; Rajan—Oh King (Parikshit); Sajjata—Awake;
Manvyah—Whose anger; Kurun—Of the Kurus; Prati—To; Udyamam—Preparations for war; Chakrur—Did; Ugrasen—By Ugrasen;
Prachoditah—Inspired. Oh king, when the Yadavas heard this news from Shri Narada, they became enraged.
King Ugrasena,
being inspired, prepared for war against the Kurus. Meaning: Srila Prabhupada writes: “Maharshi Narada immediately informed the Yadava dynasty that Samba
has been taken prisoner.”. He narrated the entire story to the Yadavas. Samba, by six warriors,
when captured in an improper manner, the Yaduvanshi became extremely angry. Therefore, after obtaining permission from Ugrasen, the chief king of the Yadu dynasty, they became eager to attack the capital of the Kuru dynasty."

Sanvativyatva tu tanramaha sannaddhanvrishnipungavan. The destroyer of the sins of the Kurus and the Vrishinas, the destroyer of the impurities of Kali. 14). Jagam Hastinapuram Rathendityavarchasa.
